{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QjQEFV2fFiBHf5AnJiwRjk","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd002-sys\n     2→title: \"pkg/sys: Syscall Abstractions\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Four utilities (ls, du, find, xargs) need OS-level capabilities that Go's standard\n     6→  library does not expose in a portable, stable form: terminal-width queries, extended\n     7→  file metadata (hard-link count, device and inode numbers, physical block counts),\n     8→  and signal handling for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH. On Darwin and Linux these syscalls\n     9→  diverge in struct layout (st_mtimespec vs st_mtim, st_blocks interpretation) and\n    10→  available interfaces (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl). Without pkg/sys each cmd/ package would\n    11→  import syscall or golang.org/x/sys directly, duplicating the Darwin/Linux divergence\n    12→  handling and making it harder to test or audit the syscall surface.\n    13→\n    14→  pkg/sys provides a stable, tested interface for these three capability areas. Each\n    15→  capability is driven by at least one concrete utility requirement derived from C source\n    16→  analysis (see dependency-map.yaml).\n    17→\n    18→goals:\n    19→  - G1: Provide a portable terminal-width query and SIGPIPE handler that work on Darwin and Linux without cgo.\n    20→  - G2: Expose extended file metadata (st_nlink, st_uid, st_gid, st_rdev, st_blocks, st_ino, st_dev) not available from os.FileInfo.\n    21→  - G3: Provide a SIGWINCH handler that calls a user-supplied callback when the terminal is resized.\n    22→\n    23→requirements:\n    24→  R1:\n    25→    title: Terminal width and TTY detection\n    26→    items:\n    27→      - R1.1: Must provide func TerminalWidth() (int, error) that returns the current terminal column count by calling ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) on stdout. Returns an error (not panic) when stdout is not a terminal or when ioctl fails.\n    28→      - R1.2: Must use golang.org/x/sys/unix (the project's only external dependency) to perform the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; must not shell out to tput or stty and must not import golang.org/x/term.\n    29→      - R1.3: |\n    30→          Must provide func IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool that returns true when the file\n    31→          descriptor refers to a terminal, and false otherwise. Implementation: call\n    32→          golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(fd), unix.TIOCGWINSZ) and return\n    33→          err == nil. Returns false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors.\n    34→          The caller (pkg/format ColorEnabled) is responsible for extracting the fd from an\n    35→          io.Writer via type assertion to *os.File. Required by pkg/format to suppress color\n    36→          output when stdout is redirected.\n    37→      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281): ls uses struct winsize to determine column count for its default multi-column layout and for the -w/--width flag default.'\n    38→      - R1.5: |\n    39→          Must provide func InstallSIGPIPEHandler() that installs a SIGPIPE handler causing the\n    40→          process to exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer.\n    41→          Implementation: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of\n    42→          size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not\n    43→          use signal.Ignore so that deferred cleanup functions do not run on SIGPIPE (matching\n    44→          GNU behavior where SIGPIPE terminates silently).\n    45→      - R1.6: InstallSIGPIPEHandler must be safe to call multiple times; only one goroutine per invocation is started.\n    46→      - R1.7: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:1600-1620): ls installs a SIGPIPE handler via sigaction so that piping ls output to head does not produce a broken-pipe error message. du, find, and xargs have the same requirement.'\n    47→\n    48→  R2:\n    49→    title: Extended file metadata\n    50→    items:\n    51→      - R2.1: Must provide func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) and func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error). Stat follows symbolic links (equivalent to os.Stat); Lstat does not follow symbolic links (equivalent to os.Lstat). Both return a *FileInfo populated from syscall.Stat_t.\n    52→      - R2.2: |\n    53→          FileInfo must be defined as the following named struct in package sys:\n    54→\n    55→            type FileInfo struct {\n    56→                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits (from syscall.Stat_t.Mode)\n    57→                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n    58→                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n    59→                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n    60→                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n    61→                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n    62→                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n    63→                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n    64→                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n    65→                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n    66→                ModTime time.Time   // modification time (st_mtime / st_mtimespec)\n    67→                Info    os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo for os package compatibility\n    68→            }\n    69→      - R2.3: Must abstract the Darwin/Linux divergence in struct field names (st_mtimespec on Darwin vs st_mtim on Linux; st_blocks interpretation) so callers use a single API regardless of target platform.\n    70→      - R2.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:213, 371): ls needs Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Mode for -l format; Blocks for -s; Ino for -i.'\n    71→      - R2.5: 'Utility context — du (du.c:554): du needs Dev and Ino for hard-link deduplication, and Blocks for physical block-count accumulation.'\n    72→      - R2.6: 'Utility context — find (find/ftsfind.c:172): find predicates (-type, -size, -newer, -perm, -uid, -gid) require Mode, Size, ModTime, Uid, Gid, Nlink from the stat struct.'\n    73→\n    74→  R3:\n    75→    title: SIGWINCH handling\n    76→    items:\n    77→      - R3.1: |\n    78→          Must provide func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that registers a SIGWINCH handler. When the terminal is resized, the handler calls TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width. If TerminalWidth() returns an error the callback is not invoked.\n    79→      - R3.2: Must support multiple calls to OnTerminalResize; each registered callback must be called on resize. Callbacks are called in registration order.\n    80→      - R3.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281, SIGWINCH registration): ls re-queries terminal width on SIGWINCH to re-format output when the terminal window is resized during a long listing.'\n    81→\n    82→non_goals:\n    83→  - pkg/sys does not wrap all of syscall or golang.org/x/sys; it exposes only the surfaces required by the current utility batch.\n    84→  - pkg/sys does not provide file-watching or inotify/kqueue interfaces.\n    85→  - pkg/sys does not handle SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 (find's debug output); those are deferred to the find PRD.\n    86→  - pkg/sys does not provide process-group or terminal control (TIOCGPGRP, tcsetpgrp); those are deferred to the xargs PRD.\n    87→\n    88→acceptance_criteria:\n    89→  - \"AC1: TerminalWidth() returns a positive integer when called in a test with a pseudo-terminal (golang.org/x/term's test helpers or os.Pipe() as a TTY substitute is acceptable).\"\n    90→  - \"AC2: Stat() and Lstat() on a known file return FileInfo with correct Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, and Blocks values as confirmed by running stat(1) on the same file.\"\n    91→  - \"AC3: Lstat() on a symlink returns the symlink's own st_mode (type bits show symlink), not the target's mode.\"\n    92→  - \"AC4: InstallSIGPIPEHandler() causes a process writing to a broken pipe to exit 0 rather than printing a write error.\"\n    93→  - \"AC5: All pkg/sys code compiles on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 with no build errors and no cgo.\"\n    94→  - \"AC6: All pkg/sys code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    95→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"000ec219-da40-4bd1-a63a-a0be702f3080","uuid":"7578c563-8b08-4834-b888-4d95e72110cc","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/228/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd002-sys.yaml","content":"id: prd002-sys\ntitle: \"pkg/sys: Syscall Abstractions\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Four utilities (ls, du, find, xargs) need OS-level capabilities that Go's standard\n  library does not expose in a portable, stable form: terminal-width queries, extended\n  file metadata (hard-link count, device and inode numbers, physical block counts),\n  and signal handling for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH. On Darwin and Linux these syscalls\n  diverge in struct layout (st_mtimespec vs st_mtim, st_blocks interpretation) and\n  available interfaces (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl). Without pkg/sys each cmd/ package would\n  import syscall or golang.org/x/sys directly, duplicating the Darwin/Linux divergence\n  handling and making it harder to test or audit the syscall surface.\n\n  pkg/sys provides a stable, tested interface for these three capability areas. Each\n  capability is driven by at least one concrete utility requirement derived from C source\n  analysis (see dependency-map.yaml).\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Provide a portable terminal-width query and SIGPIPE handler that work on Darwin and Linux without cgo.\n  - G2: Expose extended file metadata (st_nlink, st_uid, st_gid, st_rdev, st_blocks, st_ino, st_dev) not available from os.FileInfo.\n  - G3: Provide a SIGWINCH handler that calls a user-supplied callback when the terminal is resized.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Terminal width and TTY detection\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must provide func TerminalWidth() (int, error) that returns the current terminal column count by calling ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) on stdout. Returns an error (not panic) when stdout is not a terminal or when ioctl fails.\n      - R1.2: Must use golang.org/x/sys/unix (the project's only external dependency) to perform the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; must not shell out to tput or stty and must not import golang.org/x/term.\n      - R1.3: |\n          Must provide func IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool that returns true when the file\n          descriptor refers to a terminal, and false otherwise. Implementation: call\n          golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(fd), unix.TIOCGWINSZ) and return\n          err == nil. Returns false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors.\n          The caller (pkg/format ColorEnabled) is responsible for extracting the fd from an\n          io.Writer via type assertion to *os.File. Required by pkg/format to suppress color\n          output when stdout is redirected.\n      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281): ls uses struct winsize to determine column count for its default multi-column layout and for the -w/--width flag default.'\n      - R1.5: |\n          Must provide func InstallSIGPIPEHandler() that installs a SIGPIPE handler causing the\n          process to exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer.\n          Implementation: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of\n          size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not\n          use signal.Ignore so that deferred cleanup functions do not run on SIGPIPE (matching\n          GNU behavior where SIGPIPE terminates silently).\n      - R1.6: InstallSIGPIPEHandler must be safe to call multiple times; only one goroutine per invocation is started.\n      - R1.7: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:1600-1620): ls installs a SIGPIPE handler via sigaction so that piping ls output to head does not produce a broken-pipe error message. du, find, and xargs have the same requirement.'\n\n  R2:\n    title: Extended file metadata\n    items:\n      - R2.1: Must provide func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) and func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error). Stat follows symbolic links (equivalent to os.Stat); Lstat does not follow symbolic links (equivalent to os.Lstat). Both return a *FileInfo populated from syscall.Stat_t.\n      - R2.2: |\n          FileInfo must be defined as the following named struct in package sys:\n\n            type FileInfo struct {\n                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits (from syscall.Stat_t.Mode)\n                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n                ModTime time.Time   // modification time (st_mtime / st_mtimespec)\n                Info    os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo for os package compatibility\n            }\n      - R2.3: Must abstract the Darwin/Linux divergence in struct field names (st_mtimespec on Darwin vs st_mtim on Linux; st_blocks interpretation) so callers use a single API regardless of target platform.\n      - R2.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:213, 371): ls needs Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Mode for -l format; Blocks for -s; Ino for -i.'\n      - R2.5: 'Utility context — du (du.c:554): du needs Dev and Ino for hard-link deduplication, and Blocks for physical block-count accumulation.'\n      - R2.6: 'Utility context — find (find/ftsfind.c:172): find predicates (-type, -size, -newer, -perm, -uid, -gid) require Mode, Size, ModTime, Uid, Gid, Nlink from the stat struct.'\n\n  R3:\n    title: SIGWINCH handling\n    items:\n      - R3.1: |\n          Must provide func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that registers a SIGWINCH handler. When the terminal is resized, the handler calls TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width. If TerminalWidth() returns an error the callback is not invoked.\n      - R3.2: Must support multiple calls to OnTerminalResize; each registered callback must be called on resize. Callbacks are called in registration order.\n      - R3.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281, SIGWINCH registration): ls re-queries terminal width on SIGWINCH to re-format output when the terminal window is resized during a long listing.'\n\nnon_goals:\n  - pkg/sys does not wrap all of syscall or golang.org/x/sys; it exposes only the surfaces required by the current utility batch.\n  - pkg/sys does not provide file-watching or inotify/kqueue interfaces.\n  - pkg/sys does not handle SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 (find's debug output); those are deferred to the find PRD.\n  - pkg/sys does not provide process-group or terminal control (TIOCGPGRP, tcsetpgrp); those are deferred to the xargs PRD.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: TerminalWidth() returns a positive integer when called in a test with a pseudo-terminal (golang.org/x/term's test helpers or os.Pipe() as a TTY substitute is acceptable).\"\n  - \"AC2: Stat() and Lstat() on a known file return FileInfo with correct Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, and Blocks values as confirmed by running stat(1) on the same file.\"\n  - \"AC3: Lstat() on a symlink returns the symlink's own st_mode (type bits show symlink), not the target's mode.\"\n  - \"AC4: InstallSIGPIPEHandler() causes a process writing to a broken pipe to exit 0 rather than printing a write error.\"\n  - \"AC5: All pkg/sys code compiles on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 with no build errors and no cgo.\"\n  - \"AC6: All pkg/sys code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":95,"startLine":1,"totalLines":95}}}
